  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Identify the surface $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the equation of an elliptic paraboloid centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, an elliptic paraboloid centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $x$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is an elliptic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis and $b=\\sqrt{2},\\,c=2.$",
